{title: A Mighty Fortress Is Our God}
{artist: Martin Luther (1483-1546); tr. Frederick H. Hedge (1805-1890)}
{key: C}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
A [C]mighty fortress [G]is our [Am]God, a [F]bulwark never [G]fail[C]ing;
Our [C]helper He a[G]mid the [Am]flood of [F]mortal ills pre[G]vail[C]ing.
For [G]still our ancient foe doth [Am]seek to work us woe;
His [F]craft and power are great, and [G]armed with cruel hate,
On [Am]earth is [F]not [G]His [C]equal.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
Did [C]we in our own [G]strength con[Am]fide, our [F]striving would be [G]los[C]ing;
Were [C]not the right Man [G]on our [Am]side, the [F]Man of God’s own [G]choos[C]ing.
Dost [G]ask who that may be? Christ [Am]Jesus, it is He;
Lord [F]Sabaoth, His name, from [G]age to age the same,
And [Am]He must [F]win the [G]bat[C]tle.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
And [C]though this world, with [G]devils [Am]filled, should [F]threaten to un[G]do [C]us,
We [C]will not fear, for [G]God hath [Am]willed His [F]truth to triumph [G]through [C]us.
The [G]Prince of Darkness grim, we [Am]tremble not for him;
His [F]rage we can endure, for [G]lo, his doom is sure,
One [Am]little [F]word shall [G]fell [C]him.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 4}
That [C]word above all [G]earthly [Am]powers, no [F]thanks to them, a[G]bid[C]eth;
The [C]Spirit and the [G]gifts are [Am]ours through [F]Him who with us [G]sid[C]eth.
Let [G]goods and kindred go, this [Am]mortal life also;
The [F]body they may kill: God’s [G]truth abideth still,
His [Am]kingdom [F]is for[G]ev[C]er.
{eov}
